    "concept": "Digital Logic - XOR Delay Circuit & Pulse Transitions",
    "markdown": "### 1. Concept Overview\nThe circuit consists of a cascade of XOR gates and delay buffers. An XOR gate acts as an edge detector or pulse generator when one of its inputs is fed with a delayed version of the other input. For any logic signal $u(t)$, the operation $u(t) \\oplus u(t - \\delta)$ produces logic $1$ exclusively during the time windows where $u(t)$ and its delayed copy differ—namely, for a duration $\\delta$ immediately following any signal transition.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Circuit Configuration & Parameters\n* **Input Waveform $A(t)$**:\n  * $A(t) = 0$ for $t < 1\\text{ ns}$.\n  * At $t = 1\\text{ ns}$, $A(t)$ steps from $0 \\to 1$ and remains at $1$ for $t \\ge 1\\text{ ns}$.\n  * All initial internal signals and wires are stable at $0$ at $t = 0$.\n* **First Stage**:\n  * Inputs: $A(t)$ and $A(t - \\delta_1)$ where buffer delay $\\delta_1 = 2\\text{ ns}$.\n  * Intermediate output: $X(t) = A(t) \\oplus A(t - 2)$.\n* **Second Stage**:\n  * Inputs: $X(t)$ and $X(t - \\delta_2)$ where buffer delay $\\delta_2 = 4\\text{ ns}$.\n  * Final output: $B(t) = X(t) \\oplus X(t - 4)$.\n* **Gate Delays**: Both XOR gates and all interconnecting wires have zero propagation delay.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Governing Principles\nThe logic value of an XOR gate with inputs $P(t)$ and $Q(t)$ is given by:\n$$Y(t) = P(t) \\oplus Q(t) = P(t)\\overline{Q(t)} + \\overline{P(t)}Q(t)$$\nA logic transition is defined as a change of state ($0 \\to 1$ or $1 \\to 0$).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Step-by-Step Analytical Derivation\n\n#### Step 4.1: Intermediate Signal $X(t)$\n* For $t < 1\\text{ ns}$: $A(t) = 0$, $A(t - 2) = 0 \\implies X(t) = 0 \\oplus 0 = 0$.\n* At $t = 1\\text{ ns}$: $A(t)$ rises to $1$, while $A(t - 2) = 0$. Thus, $X(t) = 1 \\oplus 0 = 1$.\n* At $t = 3\\text{ ns}$: The rising edge reaches the delayed branch so $A(t - 2) = 1$. Since $A(t) = 1$, $X(t) = 1 \\oplus 1 = 0$.\n* For $t > 3\\text{ ns}$: Both inputs remain $1$, so $X(t) = 0$.\n\nHence, $X(t)$ is a single positive pulse of width $2\\text{ ns}$:\n$$X(t) = \\begin{cases} 1, & 1\\text{ ns} \\le t < 3\\text{ ns} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$\n\n#### Step 4.2: Final Output $B(t) = X(t) \\oplus X(t - 4)$\nThe delayed intermediate pulse $X(t - 4)$ is active over:\n$$1 + 4 \\le t < 3 + 4 \\implies 5\\text{ ns} \\le t < 7\\text{ ns}$$\n$$X(t - 4) = \\begin{cases} 1, & 5\\text{ ns} \\le t < 7\\text{ ns} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$\n\nSince the active intervals $[1, 3)\\text{ ns}$ and $[5, 7)\\text{ ns}$ are completely disjoint:\n1. **$0 \\le t < 1\\text{ ns}$**: $X(t) = 0, X(t - 4) = 0 \\implies B(t) = 0$.\n2. **At $t = 1\\text{ ns}$**: $X(t)$ switches $0 \\to 1 \\implies B(t)$ switches **$0 \\to 1$** (Transition 1).\n3. **At $t = 3\\text{ ns}$**: $X(t)$ switches $1 \\to 0 \\implies B(t)$ switches **$1 \\to 0$** (Transition 2).\n4. **$3\\text{ ns} \\le t < 5\\text{ ns}$**: Both are $0 \\implies B(t) = 0$.\n5. **At $t = 5\\text{ ns}$**: $X(t - 4)$ switches $0 \\to 1 \\implies B(t)$ switches **$0 \\to 1$** (Transition 3).\n6. **At $t = 7\\text{ ns}$**: $X(t - 4)$ switches $1 \\to 0 \\implies B(t)$ switches **$1 \\to 0$** (Transition 4).\n7. **$7\\text{ ns} \\le t \\le 10\\text{ ns}$**: Both are $0 \\implies B(t) = 0$.\n\nDuring the interval $[0, 10]\\text{ ns}$, transitions occur at $t = 1\\text{ ns}, 3\\text{ ns}, 5\\text{ ns},$ and $7\\text{ ns}$.\nTotal number of transitions = $4$.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Distractor Analysis\n* **Option A ($1$)**: Incorrect. Arises if one mistakenly counts only the initial rising edge of $A(t)$.\n* **Option B ($2$)**: Incorrect. Corresponds to counting only the two transitions of intermediate signal $X(t)$, neglecting the second XOR stage.\n* **Option C ($3$)**: Incorrect. Arises if one assumes overlapping cancellation between the two stages.\n* **Option D ($4$)**: **Correct**. Exactly 4 transitions ($0 \\to 1$, $1 \\to 0$, $0 \\to 1$, $1 \\to 0$) occur at $B$ at $1, 3, 5,$ and $7\\text{ ns}$.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Concluding Answer\n**Correct Answer: (D) 4**",
